In article <NAYERI.94Oct25100302@slbh13.bln.sel.alcatel.de>, nayeri@slbh13.bln.sel.alcatel.de (nader nayeri) writes:
|>Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.misc

|>Together with HP DCE a sample application is shipped called 'phone_db'. The
|>application contains the following code for error handling:
|>
|>unsigned32 pdb_print_error(unsigned32 err_num, char *prefix)
|>{
|>    int				_ignore;
|>    dce_error_string_t		dce_err_string;	/* text describing error code */
|>    char			err_buf[dce_c_error_string_len + 80];
|>    char *			cp = NULL;
|>
|>    sprintf(err_buf, "%s: ", prefix);
|>
|>    if (err_num > 512) {
|>	/* 
|>	 * Then the error must be a DCE error.
|>	 */
|>	dce_error_inq_text(err_num, dce_err_string, &_ignore);
|>	cp = (char *)dce_err_string;
|>    } else if (err_num >= 256) {
|>        cp = pdb_app_error_string(err_num);
|>
|>    } else if (err_num > 0) {
|>	/*
|>	 * This is a system errno, not a DCE error.
|>	 */
|>	cp = strerror(err_num);
|>    }
|>
|>    if (cp != NULL)
|>	strcat(err_buf, cp);
|>
|>    fprintf(stderr, "%s\n", err_buf);
|>    if (tr_handle != NULL)
|>	tr_printmsg(tr_handle, NULL, err_buf);
|>
|>    return err_num;
|>}
|>
|>This seems to me to be an elegant way to handle errors in an
|>uniform way. But would this solution also work on other UNIX platforms (SUN)? 
|>I mean, how can I be sure that the numeric constants used in the code 
|>(512, 256) are not (or are they) platform specific? Is there any way to 
|>rewrite the code in a more portable manner?

	We have a similar solution which is used by all our applications.
	Using message catalogs, we define all the common OS and some of
	the more common system errors (e.g. NFS errors) combined with
	local application errors in conjunction with end markers.  Applications 
	call library routines which then retrieves the error string or prints
	it to stderr regardless of whether it is an OS, application or DCE
	error code.

	We do have to check and make sure that the OS defined error codes
	match the ones in our message catalog when we port it to a new
	platform but this is a one time process *per* platform.  For most
	parts, the system errors seem consistent across platforms.  Using
	this simple library helped trmendeously in dealing with errors
	in applications.

								- Mustie

