In article <38ia37$4i1@hk.super.net>,
Mr Mike James Lijewski <lijewski@HK.Super.NET> wrote:
>
>Appended is a shar file of a simple DCE RPC application.  It exhibits
>what I consider very poor performance when both client and server are
>run on the same machine.  If I run one server and three clients on a
>four processor DEC Alpha 2100/500 I only see about 800 RPCs/second.
>This is no higher than if I run the server on the 2100/500 and a
>client on each of three separate workstations.  It would appear that
>the RPC runtime together with the networking code in the kernel is
>the bottleneck.  Anyone care to comment?
>

Here's a bit of information which should fan the flames a bit more.
If I compile and run a single client and server on a DEC Alpha Model
3000/800 using DEC OSF/1 V2.0 and DEC DCE V1.2A I get UDP rates of
600/second and TCP rates of 700/seconds.  If I build and run a client
and server on a four processor DEC Alpha Model 2100/500 using DEC
OSF/1 V3.0 and DEC DCE V1.3 I get UDP rates of only 450/second and
TCP rates of only 600/second.  So for this application we have a
single processor machine outperforming a supposedly faster
multiprocessor machine.  To reiterate we have at least two problems
here: 1) RPC on the same machine is slow (should be significantly
faster than using the network, but isn't); and 2) a more DEC-specific
problem that an application runs slower on a multi-processor than on
a single processor.


