In article <37olne$rqn@csugrad.cs.vt.edu> teak@csugrad.cs.vt.edu (Teak 'Teakster' Min) writes:

>   Problem:  I am working on a DEC Alpha using OSF/1.  I need to know
>   if there is anyway to #define an int in C to a long.  What I am 
>   having problems is that when I malloc and I get a memory location, 
>   the address is assigned to a pointer of type int which is 32bit but
>   the system gives back an address with is greater than 32bit address.

>   cc and gcc.  I was TOLD that c was a portable code, was I lied to?

The short answer is to use the -taso switch on the OSF/1 C compiler.
That will cause the C compiler to tell the linker to create a program
that will only have 32 bit addresses.

For the long answer I suggest that you ask about C portablility issues
in a C related newsgroup. There are lots of assumptions that you can
make in a C program that can make if non-portable.


