In <36ham6$rn2@hacgate2.hac.com> kpayne@alycia.andrew.cmu.edu writes:
>The DCE Cell Directory Service seems to be fairly robust in that
>replicas can make the CDS available even if some machines fail.
>However, as I understand it, there is one primary CDS server
>which is read-write capable.  All replicas seem to be read only.
>Is this actually true? 

Not quite.

CDS replication is at the directory level.  Each individual directory
has a master and the other copies are read-only replicas.  When you
create a directory you say which "clearinghouse" is the master.
(Each CDS server has one or more clearinghouses.  For most people's
purposes, there is only one clearinhouse per node so you can make it
easy on yourself and think "clearinhouse === CDS server machine" even
though that it not totally accurate.)

If a directory's master goes down, you can find another replica and
tell it "bump the epoch; become the master."  If all your directories
are created in the same clearinghouse, doing this will be ... painful.
	/r$


