Posted on behalf of Harold Lockhart:
------------------------------------
On October 3, without prior warning Digital Equipment Corporation, 
Hewlett-Packard Company and the Open Software Foundation announced that they 
were placing the DCE RPC and IDL compiler in the public domain.  This is a 
departure from the prior policy of licensing the DCE software in source code 
form to vendors who then provide platform-specific binary-format products to 
their customers.

The reason behind this rather startling announcement has to do with the OMG 
CORBRA 2.0 interoperability task force.  The task force is attempting to 
hammer out a standard for interoperability between Object Request Brokers 
(ORBs).  After evaluating numerous alternatives and several months of 
negotiation, the task force has lined up into two camps.  One group, that 
includes DEC and HP, strongly favors a DCE-based approach.  The other group, 
including Sun, Expersoft and ICL, favors an alternate protocol called UNO.

One objection that the UNO group has made to the use of DCE is that it is 
not freely available.  In order to counter this objection, the decision was 
made to place the DCE RPC in the public domain.

If this action succeeds in producing agreement on CORBRA 2.0 
interoperability, it will be well justified.  The alternative is quite 
undesirable.  It is well known that whatever OMG does, Microsoft intends to 
continue on its own path, adopting standards when it chooses and ignoring 
them when it chooses.  If the OMG itself becomes divided into two 
irreconcilable factions, the distributed systems market will become so 
fragmented and confused that years of progress in interoperability will have 
been lost.

However, the release of DCE RPC technology into the public domain will have 
other effects as well, some good and some potentially bad.  On the plus 
side, the availability of the source code will encourage investigation and 
experimentation with the technology.  Individuals may discover ways to 
improve the software without altering it functionally.  Others may extend 
the functionality, for example adding additional data types or 
communications semantics.  Presuming that these improvements find their way 
back into the standard product, via a PST or some other process, this will 
tend to improve DCE.  The source availability could also lead to the use of 
DCE on platforms where it is not currently available.

There are however, some possible negative consequences.  On is the 
possibility of a proliferation of non-interoperable DCE-variants.  This is 
in effect, what happened when Berkeley began to enhance UNIX.  Although 
these variants will not be able to pass the certification test suite and 
therefore will not strictly be DCE, there will be lots of people who fail to 
understand this distinction.  

This will tend to largely undo the hard work of OSF and others over the last 
several years and represents a giant step backwards in progress on 
heterogeneous interoperability.  It could in fact, lead to a general loss of 
confidence in the ability DCE to provide interoperability and its usefulness 
in general.

Another possible negative consequence stems from the fact that only the RPC 
portion of DCE, that is, one out of the five core technologies is being put 
into the public domain.  This will naturally encourage the use of RPC by 
itself, without the nameservice or security.  Yet the greatest advantage in 
the use of DCE accrues from the use of its several technologies in 
integrated fashion.  DCE RPC alone represents only a modest technological 
advance over what came before.  The five technologies taken together 
represent a real step forward.

Another point that is not addressed by the recent announcement, is what the 
future evolution will be like.  Is it just the current version (1.0.3) of 
DCE which will be public domain or do they intend to release each successive 
new version as well?  If so, when?  At the time of release from OSF? or 
perhaps after sometime lag?  I see potential problems in each of these 
scenarios.  At one extreme, we end up with a large population of obsolete, 
free versions.  At the other, we see vendors wondering why they should pay 
fees to OSF for something that is in the public domain.

Another issue is threads.  The RPC requires a Pthreads implementation to run 
over, but this component is not being released.  Presumably the assumption 
is that most systems will soon have native Pthreads implementations.  
However, DCE is based on a draft standard which has since changed.  Some 
vendors Pthreads implementations are based on newer drafts.  Also, I 
understand that DCE RPC makes use of the exception facility in DCE threads, 
that is not part of the POSIX spec.  These differences could lead to subtle 
problems and incompatibilities.

In summary, while the release of the DCE RPC into the public domain is 
justified and will produce certain benefits, there is a real risk that it 
may tend undermine some of the most important characteristics of DCE.  Most 
things in this world are incompatible.  It takes hard work and discipline to 
achieve significant interoperability, losing it is much easier.

