I think that the basic premise , that the public release of RPC/IDL
will cause functional fragmentation and that developers/users will
not know the difference between certified and uncertified versions,
to be at best mistaken fearmongering and at worst a kind of intellectual
snobbery. It would have been nice to have quoted the document in question,
inorder to point out the specific areas that I agree with or disagree with,
but the explicit copyright on the posting prohibits it. If this sounds like 
a flame... so be it. I am not impressed when someone who is speaking against
the public release of some very useful S/W , puts a copyright that requires
in toto replication of his rant. It seems too much like a paper on the 
benefits of social control ending with a signature by Benito Mussolini.
I have been using RPC (SUN and DEC) for more than 9 years. It is a
fabulous technology and I aplaud its release to the public.


